# Airline Commercial Strategist Brief

**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Lens:** Airline commercial strategy, carrier-airport contractual leverage, boarding-process economics
**Author role:** Airline-side commercial strategist testing whether any proposed MWAA standard will survive carrier response

---

## Frame

A "quiet-by-design" standard for MWAA terminals is not, from a carrier's chair, an interior-design question. It is a change to shared operating infrastructure inside preferential-use space that MWAA does not exclusively control. The public-address (PA) system, the flight-information display system (FIDS), the gate signage stack, and the boarding-choreography protocols are the interface between the airline's operational plan and the passenger. Change the interface, and you change the airline's cost curve — misconnect rate, D0 (on-time departure) performance, gate-agent workload, and IROPS-recovery labor.

The MWAA context makes this sharper. The Authority signed a new 15-year Use and Lease Agreement effective 1 January 2025 that runs to 2039 [Source: mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement]. It carries a joint capital program that has since been announced as expanding at IAD to $20–22.5B over roughly ten years [Source: avitrader.com/2026/07/31/dulles-unveils-us20bn-overhaul]. Signatory carriers — dominated by United at IAD (hub) and American at DCA (53% market share) [Source: simpleflying.com/american-airlines-washington-dc-routes-analysis-march-2025/] — sit at the other side of every rate-setting and capital-approval conversation. Any quiet-by-design standard that changes the technology stack inside airline preferential-use space is a candidate for majority-in-interest (MII) scrutiny.

The context packet flagged that specific MII thresholds under the 2025 U&L were not independently verified. That gap is load-bearing for this brief and should be closed before the recommendation hardens.

The central test I apply below is the same test I apply to every airport strategy: **what will the carriers do in response?**

---

## Key findings

1. **IAD cost-per-enplanement (CPE) is on a rising trajectory before quiet-by-design adds a dollar.** IAD's 2024 CPE is $12.77–$12.88, and MWAA's own 2025 bond Official Statement projects $20.71 by 2030 [Source: bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. A May 2026 airline briefing on the full expansion projected $90.64 by 2035 [Source: crankyflier.com/2026/08/04/the-plan-for-dulles-is-ambitious-and-ridiculous-and-it-wont-happen/]. Any incremental capital load — including quiet-by-design AV, acoustic materials, and gate-audio zoning — enters a CPE environment approaching or exceeding today's JFK ($36.01) and SFO ($32.14) levels. That is the environment inside which airline signatories will vote MII on quiet-by-design line items.

2. **The peer benchmarks in the run prompt are structurally not comparable to MWAA on carrier concentration.** Schiphol (KLM/Air France dominant), Dubai (Emirates dominant), Copenhagen (SAS-anchored), Helsinki (Finnair-dominant), and London City (BA/CityFlyer core) each pair a state-adjacent or single-carrier-dominant network with an airport-of-use operator that sets tenant rules unilaterally in a way MWAA cannot. MWAA governs by contract with a multi-carrier signatory group. The comparable question is not "can MWAA copy AMS?" but "which parts of the AMS/DXB standard would American and United agree to in a signatory-airline vote?"

3. **DCA and IAD have opposite operational cadences and the same standard will fit one worse.** American runs DCA as an O&D-heavy, short-haul, high-frequency operation — up to 300+ daily departures, with the passenger arriving 60–90 minutes before departure and connecting less. United runs IAD as a connecting complex with international banks whose misconnect economics dominate the day. A silent-airport rule that suppresses gate-area announcements outside the immediate boarding zone hits an IAD international bank differently than a DCA shuttle. The MWAA standard must specify by terminal and by operating context or it will be fought carrier-by-carrier.

4. **SFO's quiet policy has been in force since 2018 and boarding-announcement restrictions since 2020, with no publicly reported airline pushback on missed-boarding rates.** SFO reports a 40% reduction in announcements in boarding areas and roughly 90 minutes per day removed from the International Terminal [Source: simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/]. Airlines have not withdrawn service or filed public complaints. But the SFO case is a single-hub-carrier environment (United ~45% of departures) and does not test the multi-hub-carrier dynamic MWAA lives inside.

5. **The airline-side alignment on quiet-by-design is available, but only if the standard preserves boarding-choreography and IROPS-communication rights at the gate.** Airlines have already invested heavily in mobile push (United's app pushes gate holds; AA's automated hold-flight system averages 10-minute holds and notifies affected passengers via text [Source: viewfromthewing.com/3-cases-where-american-airlines-gate-agents-may-hold-the-boarding-door-for-passengers/]). Push-first works commercially for the airline. What the airline will not surrender is the option to make an announcement at the gate when its own systems fail or when a specific operational recovery requires spoken direction to a specific passenger group.

6. **NFPA 72 and DOT ADA effectively floor the standard.** Any quiet-by-design specification must clear NFPA 72's Speech Transmission Index (STI) ≥ 0.50 for emergency voice/alarm communications in required areas and the DOT ADA rule (49 CFR Part 27) requirement for effective communication with redundant visual and assistive channels. These are federal floors below which no operator or airline can go. The standard should be written as "we will meet the federal floors and specify above them"; not as "we will design for quiet and confirm compliance later."

7. **The airline-facing risk with the biggest cost tail is a standard that mandates a specific vendor technology (AviaVox-style intelligent announcement, directional speaker arrays, addressable gate-audio zones) inside preferential-use space.** That is where MII teeth are sharpest. A performance-based standard (STI, ambient dBA, adjacent-zone spillover) that leaves technology selection to the airline is far easier to get through a signatory vote than a prescriptive one.

8. **The one MWAA-specific commercial fact worth naming: United's public posture on the $22.5B Dulles expansion is guarded, not enthusiastic.** United CEO Scott Kirby appeared "not too disturbed in the background" of the July 2026 announcement — cranky-flier read that as strategic calculation to extract Concourse E and defer the rest [Source: crankyflier.com/2026/08/04/the-plan-for-dulles-is-ambitious-and-ridiculous-and-it-wont-happen/]. If United treats the wider expansion as optional, MWAA's runway to load a quiet-by-design capital standard into the same MII vote is narrow. The standard has to be small enough at the margin to pass alongside things United actually wants.

