## Evidence and mechanisms

### The Use and Lease Agreement determines what MWAA can do unilaterally

The 2025 U&L was executed as a hybrid/residual-style agreement covering both IAD and DCA and runs through 2039 [Source: mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement]. Under U.S. residual/hybrid-airline-agreement convention, MII clauses typically require a supermajority of signatory-airline landed weight or gate count to approve capital projects above a defined threshold within specific project categories (terminal, airfield, ancillary). The 2025 U&L's specific MII thresholds and categories are not in the public summary. Any thesis that assumes MWAA can specify audio infrastructure inside preferential-use space without a signatory-airline vote is untested until the U&L text is read.

Practical implication: quiet-by-design should be written so that its **mandatory** requirements sit inside operator common-use space (curbfront, non-secure headhouse, sterile corridor between checkpoint and concourse, immigration hall, baggage claim) and its **airline-negotiated** requirements sit at the gate and inside preferential-use holdrooms. This distinction is the difference between a standard that MWAA can publish on Monday and a standard that has to be negotiated for six quarters.

### Carrier concentration at each airport shapes the negotiating posture

**IAD.** United is the hub carrier and executed a separate long-term lease with MWAA in the 2024–2025 cycle [Source: mwaa.com/news/airports-authority-and-united-airlines-execute-long-term-lease-dulles-international]. United is the counterparty that matters on Concourse E, the demolition of the mobile lounges, the C/D replacement, and the extended AeroTrain [Source: avitrader.com/2026/07/31/dulles-unveils-us20bn-overhaul]. Foreign-flag Star Alliance partners (Lufthansa, Air Canada, ANA, others) matter for international-bank connection timing but have limited MII leverage as smaller signatories. On PA and gate-audio specification, IAD is effectively a bilateral negotiation with United, coordinated with Star Alliance service standards.

**DCA.** American holds 53% share [Source: simpleflying.com/american-airlines-washington-dc-routes-analysis-march-2025/] and moved ~14 million passengers in and out of DCA last year. Delta, Southwest, Alaska, and JetBlue split the remainder, with each receiving new beyond-perimeter slots under the 2024 FAA Reauthorization Act. DCA is therefore a bilateral negotiation with American with meaningful minority-carrier voice — closer to a multi-signatory environment than IAD.

**Standard-writing implication.** The same PA-zoning rule at DCA will be negotiated by American in a room with four other signatories who each got new slots and want their gate experience to look like their home hub. That is a harder negotiation than IAD's, not an easier one.

### CPE is the compass for airline appetite

MWAA's own filings project IAD CPE roughly doubling from ~$12.77 in 2024 to $20.71 by 2030 without the July 2026 expansion added [Source: bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. John Van Yperen (Aviation Financial Intelligence Database) put it directly: *"Roughly doubling of airline cost per passenger at Dulles is already in MWAA's own published forecast before a dollar of the new program is added to it"* [Source: bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions].

Comparative context: JFK $36.01, SFO $32.14, EWR $31.67, LAX $30.16, ORD $29.56 [Source: crankyflier.com/2026/08/04/the-plan-for-dulles-is-ambitious-and-ridiculous-and-it-wont-happen/]. IAD is on a path toward the top half of that list before quiet-by-design specification adds anything. On the AA side at DCA, the airport's CPE is broadly in the low-to-mid teens and American treats DCA as one of its most profitable hubs [Source: simpleflying.com/american-airlines-washington-dc-routes-analysis-march-2025/] — profitable enough that a small incremental CPE for a well-scoped standard will not trigger a route response, but large enough that a poorly scoped one will.

**Rule of thumb from the field:** carriers begin diverting growth from a hub when its CPE rises materially above network-median CPE without a matched revenue-per-enplanement uplift. IAD is not there yet on 2024 numbers but is trending. Quiet-by-design should be scoped so that its incremental CPE contribution is measured in cents, not dollars.

### Boarding choreography is a real cost line for the airline

American Airlines uses gate-hold decisions with 10-minute average hold windows and automated SMS notifications to affected passengers [Source: viewfromthewing.com/missed-connections-why-some-airlines-hold-the-plane-for-you-but-others-wont/]. United's app pushes gate changes and holds. Every carrier boards on a schedule that closes the door 10–15 minutes before departure and treats D0 as a core KPI.

Two mechanisms from that reality:

1. **The airline's tolerance for reduced gate-area announcement volume is high if the passenger-facing information channel is redundant and reliable.** The airline benefits when its passengers self-manage through mobile push, because it lowers gate-agent workload and improves boarding-pace predictability.

2. **The airline's tolerance drops sharply during IROPS.** When the app goes down, when a gate changes twice in twenty minutes, when a diversion cascade needs to be re-boarded, the gate agent's PA is the failsafe. A quiet-by-design standard that removes the gate-PA option — as opposed to constraining its default use — will lose the airline in negotiation.

### SFO is the closest domestic case, and it is closer to IAD than to DCA

SFO's Quiet Airport program has been in force since 2018 with gate-area announcement restrictions layered on in 2020. Airlines make boarding calls only in the immediate gate area. SFO reports a 40% reduction in boarding-area announcements and ~90 minutes/day removed from the International Terminal [Source: simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/]. No United withdrawal of service, no public airline complaint, and simpleflying's coverage explicitly attributes viability in part to *"United Airlines, SFO's largest carrier, [having] a robust app with push notifications"* [Source: simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/].

SFO's fit is closest to IAD (single-hub carrier, international-heavy, connecting complex). It is a much weaker precedent for DCA, where the carrier profile is more diverse and short-haul.

**Conflict worth naming:** vendor and trade-press coverage of "silent airports" repeatedly cites Schiphol, Helsinki, and Dubai as models without engaging with the fact that each is a single-dominant-carrier airport. If MWAA argues its DCA program by analogy to AMS, an airline commercial team will note the mismatch in the first meeting.

