## Case studies of carrier response to airport decisions on shared infrastructure

1. **SFO Quiet Airport (2018–present).** SFO installed the policy incrementally with United as its dominant hub carrier already committed to mobile push. Airline complaints have been muted; passenger complaints have been public but low-cost to the airport. **Read for MWAA:** works when the airport has one hub carrier and that carrier's app is a credible substitute channel. This is the IAD/United case, not the DCA/five-carrier case.

2. **AMS Schiphol silent-airport model.** Schiphol implemented near-elimination of announcements with KLM alignment [Source: simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/; general reporting]. KLM is the operator's structural partner. The lesson is that airline alignment is the enabling condition, not the trailing consequence. **Read for MWAA:** you cannot lead with the standard and expect airline alignment to follow; the alignment is the standard.

3. **DXB Dubai International silent-airport rollout.** Emirates is the dominant carrier and the airport is state-owned. There is no MII vote in Dubai. **Read for MWAA:** be cautious about lifting operational rules from an environment without contractual counterparties.

4. **CVG Delta dehubbing (2005–2013) — the mechanism, not the outcome.** Delta pulled 70%+ of its CVG capacity in stages after CPE and connecting-yield economics failed the network test. The airport had just completed capital that the carrier no longer wanted to pay for. **Read for MWAA:** the airport's fate is more determined by carrier decisions than by anything the airport itself does. Any standard that raises carrier cost meaningfully at the margin must be tested against the carrier's willingness to keep paying it over the 15-year U&L term.

5. **United's guarded posture on the $22.5B Dulles plan.** Kirby's public body language and the trade-press read (cranky-flier's July 2026 assessment that the full plan "won't happen") is a live commercial signal [Source: crankyflier.com/2026/08/04/the-plan-for-dulles-is-ambitious-and-ridiculous-and-it-wont-happen/]. **Read for MWAA:** you have less capital-decision runway with United than the joint announcement suggests. Do not load a quiet-by-design standard into the same MII vote as the mobile-lounge demolition unless the standard's incremental cost is trivial or the standard's operational benefit to United is undeniable.

