## Evidence gaps a downstream stage should close

1. The 2025 U&L executed text — specifically the MII thresholds, project-approval categories, and the signatory-airline list. Every recommendation in this brief presumes airline consent is required for infrastructure inside preferential-use space; the U&L text will confirm or narrow that.
2. Whether MWAA's Design Manual currently specifies acoustic performance (ambient dBA, reverberation time, STI) at the terminal level and, if so, at what values.
3. Whether the current MWAA airline agreement's Common-Use Passenger Processing System (CUPPS) technology addendum treats gate-audio zoning as airline preferential or airport common. This determines whether MWAA can specify the audio stack at the gate under the U&L or must negotiate it.
4. Data — from SFO or from AviaVox-installed airports — on measurable change in missed-boarding rate or D0 performance after quiet-airport rollout. None of the public reporting produces that number, and it is the number that would settle the airline argument.

