## The airline commercial veto, applied to this thesis

The question is not whether a quiet-by-design standard is a good idea in principle. It is whether the standard MWAA publishes will still be intact after American's contracts team, United's real-estate team, and the DCA minority-signatory group have all made their MII marks on it.

The standard that survives is:

- Performance-based, not vendor-prescriptive.
- Written above federal floors (NFPA 72 STI ≥ 0.50, DOT ADA 49 CFR Part 27) with the floors named.
- Mandatory in operator common-use space; negotiated in airline preferential-use space.
- Differentiated by terminal and operating context, with a written airline-signatory concurrence path.
- Scoped so its incremental CPE contribution is small enough to pass alongside things carriers already want.
- Enforced through the U&L amendment cycle and the Design Manual, not through unilateral operator directive.

The standard that fails is a single prescriptive MWAA-wide rule that specifies vendors and technology inside airline preferential-use space, is uniform across IAD and DCA, and enters MII scrutiny attached to the same capital envelope as the mobile-lounge demolition.

The Council should test every proposed requirement against one question: **will American and United both sign it in the signatory-airline vote?** If the answer is no, the requirement is not a standard. It is an aspiration.
